  data: {
    LLM: 'Game: Kaiser. Logic: See rules.objective and rules.gameplay.',
    Player: "Kaiser is a beloved Canadian trick-taking game, historically popular in Saskatchewan and the Prairie provinces. Played by four players in partnerships, it uses a unique 32-card deck featuring two highly consequential cards: the 'Kaiser' (3 of Spades, worth -3 points) and the 'Bonus card' (5 of Hearts, worth +5 points). It is a game of high variance and strategic 'dumping' of negative points.",
    objective: 'Be the first team to reach +52 points (or reach the bid total).',
    gameplay: '1. Must follow suit. If unable, play any card.\n2. Highest trump wins; if no trump, highest card of led suit wins.\n3. **Scoring**: Each trick = 1 pt. 5 of Hearts = +5 pts. 3 of Spades = -3 pts.',
    keyRules: [
      'Must follow suit if able; if unable, may play any card.',
      'Highest trump wins the trick; if no trump, highest card of led suit wins.',
      'Each trick worth 1 point; 5 of Hearts +5, 3 of Spades -3 to the side that takes them.',
      'First team to reach 52 or more points wins. Bidding team must make their bid or lose the bid amount.',
